API Support**: Add OpenAI API as an alternative provider for appropriate tasks\\n4. **Quality Maintenance**: Ensure delegation decisions maintain output quality and system functionality\\n\\n## Core Requirements\\n\\n### 1. Research & Documentation (Phase 1)\\n\\n**Research Document Deliverable**:\\n- Analyze current Anthropic API usage patterns across all agent types\\n- Document which agent tasks can operate autonomously (no user interaction needed)\\n- Research local Claude deployment options (ollama, local API servers, etc.)\\n- Evaluate OpenAI model capabilities vs current Claude usage\\n- Define cost comparison matrix between providers\\n- Create decision framework for model selection based on task complexity and autonomy level\\n- Document implementation approach and architecture changes needed\\n\\n**Agent Documentation Audit**:\\n- Review all 40+ agent definitions to identify:\\n  - Which agents perform fully autonomous tasks\\n  - Current model requirements and complexity ratings\\n  - Tasks suitable for local/cheaper models\\n  - Critical tasks requiring premium models\\n\\n### 2. OpenAI API Integration (Phase 2)\\n\\n**Technical Implementation**:\\n- Extend `runtime.py` to support OpenAI API alongside Anthropic\\n- Modify `model_selector.py` to include OpenAI models in selection logic\\n- Update `cost_calculator.py` for OpenAI pricing\\n- Add OpenAI configuration to environment management\\n- Implement fallback mechanisms (OpenAI \\u2192 Anthropic \\u2192 Local Claude)\\n\\n**Model Mapping**:\\n- Map agent complexity levels to appropriate OpenAI models (GPT-4, GPT-3.5-turbo, etc.)\\n- Update budget tier system to include OpenAI options\\n- Implement dynamic model selection based on task autonomy and complexity\\n\\n### 3. Local Claude Integration (Phase 3)\\n\\n**Local Provider Detection**:\\n- Auto-detect available local Claude instances (check common ports, endpoints)\\n- Implement health checks for local providers\\n- Create configuration system for local endpoint URLs\\n\\n**Task Delegation Logic**:\\n- Define criteria for local delegation:\\n  - Task can complete autonomously (no user input required)\\n  - Task complexity is low-to-medium\\n  - Local instance is healthy and responsive\\n- Implement intelligent routing: Local \\u2192 OpenAI \\u2192 Anthropic\\n\\n### 4. Cost Optimization Framework\\n\\n**Smart Model Selection**:\\n- Dynamic model selection based on:\\n  - Task autonomy level (fully autonomous vs requires user input)\\n  - Agent type and complexity rating\\n  - Available provider health status\\n  - Cost optimization goals vs quality requirements\\n- Implement cost tracking and reporting per provider\\n\\n**Quality Safeguards**:\\n- Automatic quality assessment of local/cheaper model outputs\\n- Fallback to premium models if quality metrics fail\\n- A/B testing framework to validate cost optimizations maintain quality\\n\\n## Technical Architecture\\n\\n### Current State Analysis\\n- **Runtime System**: `/src/runtime/agents/runtime.py` - Anthropic-only implementation\\n- **Model Selection**: `/src/runtime/agents/model_selector.py` - Claude model selection only\\n- **Cost Tracking**: `/src/runtime/agents/cost_calculator.py` - Anthropic pricing only\\n- **Agent Definitions**: 40+ agents with varying complexity and autonomy levels\\n\\n### Proposed Architecture\\n```\\nCost Optimization Layer\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Provider Manager\\n\\u2502   \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 AnthropicProvider (existing)\\n\\u2502   \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 OpenAIProvider (new)\\n\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 LocalClaudeProvider (new)\\n\\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Smart Router\\n\\u2502   \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Task Analyzer (autonomy detection)\\n\\u2502   \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Provider Selector (cost/quality optimization)\\n\\u2502   \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 Fallback Handler\\n\\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 Quality Monitor\\n    \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Output Validator\\n    \\u251c\\u2500\\u2500 Cost Tracker (multi-provider)\\n    \\u2514\\u2500\\u2500 Performance Reporter\\n```\\n\\n## Success Criteria\\n\\n### Functional Requirements\\n1. **Provider Support**: System supports Anthropic, OpenAI, and local Claude providers\\n2. **Automatic Routing**: Tasks automatically route to most cost-effective suitable provider\\n3. **Quality Maintenance**: Output quality metrics remain within 95% of baseline\\n4. **Cost Reduction**: Achieve 30-50% cost reduction on autonomous tasks\\n5. **Fallback Reliability**: Seamless fallback when preferred providers fail\\n\\n### Technical Requirements\\n1. **Backward Compatibility**: Existing agent definitions work without modification\\n2. **Configuration Flexibility**: Easy provider enablement/disablement via config\\n3. **Monitoring**: Comprehensive cost and quality tracking across providers\\n4. **Documentation**: Clear setup instructions for each provider type\\n\\n## Out of Scope\\n\\n- **Training Custom Models**: Focus on existing model APIs only\\n- **Agent Definition Modifications**: Avoid changing agent behaviors/capabilities\\n- **UI Changes**: Backend optimization only (UI changes come later)\\n- **Real-time Model Switching**: Single model per agent execution (no mid-task switching)\\n\\n## Constraints & Assumptions\\n\\n### Technical Constraints\\n- Must maintain existing agent hierarchy and spawn permissions\\n- Cannot break existing test suite\\n- Must preserve current debugging and metrics capabilities\\n\\n### Business Constraints\\n- No breaking changes to user-facing functionality\\n- Implementation must be reversible (can disable new providers)\\n- Quality cannot degrade below acceptable thresholds\\n\\n### Assumptions\\n- User has or can obtain OpenAI API credentials\\n- Local Claude instances (when available) have compatible APIs\\n- Cost savings justify implementation complexity\\n- Autonomous tasks can be reliably identified programmatically\\n\\n## Implementation Approach\\n\\n### Research First Strategy\\n1. **Phase 1**: Comprehensive research document with findings and recommendations\\n2. **Phase 2**: OpenAI integration as proof-of-concept for multi-provider approach\\n3. **Phase 3**: Local Claude integration based on research findings\\n4. **Phase 4**: Optimization and quality validation\\n\\n### Risk Mitigation\\n- Implement feature flags for gradual rollout\\n- Maintain detailed logging for debugging provider issues\\n- Create rollback procedures for each integration phase\\n- Establish quality baselines before implementing changes\\n\\n## Deliverables\\n\\n### Research Phase\\n- **Research Document**: Comprehensive analysis and implementation recommendations\\n- **Cost Analysis**: Provider comparison matrix with pricing projections\\n- **Agent Audit**: Documentation of autonomy levels and provider suitability per agent\\n- **Architecture Design**: Detailed technical design for multi-provider support\\n\\n### Implementation Phase\\n- **Provider Integration**: Working OpenAI and local Claude provider support\\n- **Smart Router**: Intelligent task-to-provider mapping system\\n- **Monitoring System**: Cost and quality tracking across providers\\n- **Documentation**: Setup guides and configuration reference\\n- **Test Suite**: Comprehensive tests for multi-provider scenarios\\n\\n## Notes & Context\\n\\n- User submitted via web UI seeking cost optimization for the ensemble system\\n- Current system has 40+ specialized agents with varying complexity levels\\n- Focus on autonomous task delegation to preserve user experience\\n- OpenAI integration serves as stepping stone to broader provider ecosystem\\n- Research document should inform implementation priorities and approaches\\n\\""}'}]}], 'model': 'claude-3-5-haiku-20241022', 'system': 'You are a System Architect agent.\n\n## Purpose\nDesigns the show formations and execution strategy. Designs software architecture based on requirements documents. Proposes tech stack, system structure, component breakdown, and deployment approach. Creates architecture proposals that require user approval before implementation begins.\n\n## Instructions\nYou are a software architect. Your job is to design a robust, maintainable architecture based on requirements. Your proposal will be reviewed by the user before implementation begins.\n\n### Your Process:\n\n**BE DECISIVE**: Make architectural decisions based on industry best practices. Only escalate when major trade-offs exist (e.g., SQL vs NoSQL for specific use case with conflicting requirements).\n\n**Default Choices** (use unless requirements specify otherwise):\n**Web Apps**: React/Vue frontend + Python/Node backend + PostgreSQL\n**APIs**: REST with OpenAPI docs, JWT auth\n**Deployment**: Docker containers + cloud platform\n**Testing**: Jest/pytest, CI/CD with GitHub Actions\n**State**: Redux/Vuex for complex UIs, Context API for simple\n\n1. **Read Requirements**: Use read_file to thoroughly understand the requirements document\n\n2. **Analyze Constraints**: Consider:\nFunctional requirements (what must it do?)\nNon-functional requirements (performance, scale, security)\nTechnical constraints (languages, platforms, existing systems)\nResource constraints (time, complexity, cost)\n\n3. **Design Architecture**: Create a comprehensive proposal with these sections:\n\n   **a) Architecture Overview**\nHigh-level system design\nArchitecture pattern (e.g., MVC, microservices, layered)\nRationale for chosen approach\n\n   **b) Tech Stack**\nLanguages and frameworks\nLibraries and dependencies\nTools and platforms\n**For each choice, explain WHY and what alternatives were considered**\n\n   **c) System Components**\nComponent breakdown\nResponsibility of each component\nHow components interact\nData flow diagrams (in text/markdown)\n\n   **d) File/Directory Structure**\nProposed directory layout\nModule organization\nConfiguration file locations\n\n   **e) Data Model** (if applicable)\nDatabase schema\nData structures\nState management approach\n\n   **f) API Design** (if applicable)\nEndpoint structure\nRequest/response formats\nAuthentication approach\n\n   **g) Deployment Strategy**\nHow will this be deployed?\nEnvironment configuration\nCI/CD considerations\n\n   **h) Testing Strategy**\nUnit testing approach\nIntegration testing approach\nHow to verify requirements are met\n\n   **i) Alternatives Considered**\nWhat other approaches were considered?\nWhy were they rejected?\nTrade-offs of chosen approach\n\n   **j) Risks and Mitigations**\nPotential risks in this architecture\nHow to mitigate them\n\n   **k) Open Questions**\nDecisions that need user input\nTrade-offs where user preference matters\n\n4. **Write the Proposal**: Use write_file to create a clear, comprehensive markdown document\n\n5. **Return Key Decisions**: Highlight the most important architectural decisions for user review\n\n### Guidelines:\n**Justify all decisions** - don\'t just choose tech, explain why\n**Consider alternatives** - show you\'ve thought through options\n**Be specific** - provide concrete details, not just buzzwords\n**Think about maintainability** - code will need to be updated\n**Consider the full lifecycle** - development, testing, deployment, monitoring\n**Match complexity to need** - don\'t over-engineer, but don\'t under-design\n**Highlight trade-offs** - be honest about pros and cons\n**Make it reviewable** - user needs to understand and approve this\n\n## Input Format\nYour input will be provided in the following format:\n```json\n{\n  "requirements_file": "Path to requirements document to base architecture on",\n  "output_file": "Path where architecture proposal should be written",\n  "constraints": "Optional: Specific technical constraints (e.g., must use Python, deploy on AWS)"\n}\n```\n\n## Output Format\nYou must respond with JSON matching this exact format:\n```json\n{\n  "status": "success|needs_clarification",\n  "architecture_file": "Path to the written architecture proposal",\n  "message": "Summary of architectural approach",\n  "key_decisions": ["List of major architectural decisions for user review"],\n  "self_analysis": "string - REQUIRED: Your performance analysis (2-4 sentences, see Self-Improvement Directive)"\n}\n```\n\n## When to Request Clarification\nRequest clarification when:\n\n\n## Termination Conditions\nYou are done when:\n- Complete architecture proposal has been written\n- Tech stack is justified with trade-offs explained\n- File structure and component breakdown are defined\n- Data flow is documented\n- Alternative approaches are considered\n\nCRITICAL: Your response must be valid JSON matching the Output Format exactly.\n\n## Your Performance History\n- Success rate: 94.3%\n- Average iterations per task: 3.4\n- Common errors to avoid: CircuitBreakerOpenError, RateLimitError\n\n**Tips for this run:**\n- Watch out for these common errors: CircuitBreakerOpenError, RateLimitError\n- Great work! Maintain your high performance standards.\n\n**Learnings from your recent runs:**\n- The architecture successfully translates the milestone requirements into a robust, flexible design. By emphasizing modular components, cross-platform compatibility, and minimal performance impact, the...\n- Successfully transformed a simple model validation requirement into a comprehensive, scientifically rigorous architecture. The design balances statistical validity with practical constraints, providin...\n', 'tools': [{'name': 'write_file', 'description': 'Write content to a file, creating parent directories if needed. Automatically backs up existing files.', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to write'}, 'content': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Content to write to the file'}}, 'required': ['file_path', 'content']}}, {'name': 'read_file', 'description': 'Read content from a file', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'file_path': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Path to the file to read'}}, 'required': ['file_path']}}, {'name': 'run_command', 'description': 'Execute a shell command and return the output', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'command': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'The shell command to execute'}, 'working_directory': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Optional working directory for command execution'}}, 'required': ['command']}}, {'name': 'git_commit', 'description': 'Commit changes to the git repository with a descriptive message', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Commit message describing the changes'}, 'files': {'type': 'array', 'items': {'type': 'string'}, 'description': 'Optional list of specific files to commit. If not provided, commits all modified files.'}}, 'required': ['message']}}, {'name': 'git_branch', 'description': 'Create, switch, list, or delete git branches for coordinated development', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create', 'switch', 'list', 'delete', 'current'], 'description': 'Action to perform: create new branch, switch to existing, list all, delete, or get current'}, 'branch_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Name of the branch (for create, switch, delete)'}, 'from_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Base branch to create from (for create, defaults to current branch)'}, 'force': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Force the operation (for delete unmerged branches)', 'default': False}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_merge', 'description': 'Merge branches or create pull requests for code review', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'preview', 'abort'], 'description': 'Action: merge branches, preview changes, or abort an in-progress merge'}, 'source_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge from'}, 'target_branch': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Branch to merge into (defaults to current branch)'}, 'strategy': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['merge', 'squash', 'rebase'], 'description': 'Merge strategy (default: merge)', 'default': 'merge'}, 'message': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Custom merge commit message (optional)'}, 'no_ff': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Create merge commit even if fast-forward is possible', 'default': True}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'git_status', 'description': 'Get comprehensive git repository status including branches, changes, and history', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'include_history': {'type': 'boolean', 'description': 'Include recent commit history', 'default': False}, 'history_count': {'type': 'integer', 'description': 'Number of commits to include in history', 'default': 10}}, 'required': []}}, {'name': 'project_tracking', 'description': 'Manage project state, tasks, and notes for the current project', 'input_schema': {'type': 'object', 'properties': {'action': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['create_project', 'add_task', 'update_task', 'add_note', 'get_summary', 'get_next_tasks'], 'description': 'Action to perform'}, 'project_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project ID (not needed for create_project)'}, 'project_name': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Project name (for create_project)'}, 'description': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Description (for create_project or add_task)'}, 'task_id': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Task ID (for update_task)'}, 'status': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['todo', 'in_progress', 'completed', 'blocked', 'cancelled'], 'description': 'Task status (for update_task)'}, 'note': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Note text (for add_note or update_task)'}, 'assigned_to': {'type': 'string', 'description': 'Agent type (for add_task)'}, 'category': {'type': 'string', 'enum': ['general', 'decision', 'milestone', 'issue'], 'description': 'Note category (for add_note)'}}, 'required': ['action']}}, {'name': 'spawn_agent', 'description': 'Spawn and execute a s